  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Euler’s Formula",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert between $e^{j\\theta}$ and $\\cos\\theta + j\\sin\\theta$ using Euler’s formula to connect complex exponential form with sinusoidal real and imaginary parts in continuous-time signals.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Euler’s Formula",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Euler’s Formula",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Euler's formula is the bridge between complex exponential notation and rectangular form. It tells you that $e^{j\\theta}$ can be rewritten as $\\cos\\theta+j\\sin\\theta$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\theta}=\\cos\\theta+j\\sin\\theta",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $\\theta=0$ as the anchor case. Then both sides collapse to $1$, so the identity is consistent in the simplest possible case.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j0}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Once the identity is in place, read the parts directly: the real part is $\\cos\\theta$ and the imaginary part is $\\sin\\theta$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the conversion rule that later examples will reuse when they move between exponential form and sinusoidal components in continuous-time signals.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep that bridge in mind: the same expression can be treated either as $e^{j\\theta}$ or as $\\cos\\theta+j\\sin\\theta$ depending on which form is more useful.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Euler’s Formula",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Converting $e^{j\\theta}$ to $\\cos\\theta + j\\sin\\theta$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $e^{j\\pi/3}$ to rectangular form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use Euler's formula as the conversion rule. The cue is the complex exponential $e^{j\\pi/3}$, so rewrite it as $\\cos(\\pi/3)+j\\sin(\\pi/3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\pi/3}=\\cos(\\pi/3)+j\\sin(\\pi/3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the exact unit-circle values. Since $\\cos(\\pi/3)=\\tfrac12$ and $\\sin(\\pi/3)=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, the expression becomes a rectangular complex number.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\pi/3)+j\\sin(\\pi/3)=\\tfrac12+j\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the result is $\\tfrac12+j\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$. The real part is $\\tfrac12$ and the imaginary part is $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, which is exactly the rectangular form we wanted.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Euler’s Formula",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Converting $\\cos\\theta + j\\sin\\theta$ to $e^{j\\theta}$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $\\cos(\\pi/6)+j\\sin(\\pi/6)$ as a single complex exponential.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use Euler's formula as the recognition rule. The cue is the cosine-plus-sine pair $\\cos(\\pi/6)+j\\sin(\\pi/6)$, so rewrite it as the matching exponential $e^{j\\pi/6}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\pi/6)+j\\sin(\\pi/6)=e^{j\\pi/6}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the angle in both trig terms exactly. Because both terms use $\\pi/6$, the pair is already Euler's formula written in rectangular form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\pi/6}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the result is $e^{j\\pi/6}$. The same complex number is now expressed in the shorter exponential form, which is the point of the conversion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Euler’s Formula",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Applying Euler’s Formula at Specific Angles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $e^{j\\pi/2}$ to rectangular form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use Euler's formula as the conversion rule. The cue is the special-angle exponential $e^{j\\pi/2}$, so rewrite it as $\\cos(\\pi/2)+j\\sin(\\pi/2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\pi/2}=\\cos(\\pi/2)+j\\sin(\\pi/2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the exact unit-circle values. Since $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$ and $\\sin(\\pi/2)=1$, the expression collapses immediately.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\pi/2)+j\\sin(\\pi/2)=j",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the result is $j$. This special angle lands on the imaginary axis, which is why the angle choice matters.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Euler’s Formula",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Connecting Euler’s Formula to Real and Imaginary Sinusoidal Parts",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$, identify the real and imaginary parts of $x(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use Euler's formula as the conversion rule. The cue is the complex exponential signal $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$, so rewrite it as $\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now read off the parts directly. The real part is $\\cos(\\omega t)$ and the imaginary part is $\\sin(\\omega t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{x(t)\\}=\\cos(\\omega t),\\qquad \\Im\\{x(t)\\}=\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal can be viewed either as a complex exponential or as its sinusoidal real and imaginary components. This is the signal-processing interpretation that later examples will reuse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
